<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scramble for Africa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a>–<a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Scramble for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> was the rapid partition and colonisation of most of the African continent by European powers in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. It was given diplomatic form by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a> of 1884–1885. It is discussed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> profiles and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> profile, which describes the British conquest that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>'s independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa.jpg" alt="Scramble for Africa" width="320" height="298" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Israel (ZyMOS) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:African-civilizations-map-pre-colonial.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By 1841 European businessmen had set up small trading posts along the African coasts, but they seldom moved inland, and large parts of the continent were close to uninhabitable for Europeans because of tropical diseases such as malaria. As late as the 1870s Europeans controlled about 10 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, all of it near the coasts, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, held by Portugal, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, held by the United Kingdom, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Steamships, railways, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraphs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quinine">quinine</a> made the interior far more accessible.</p><p>During the Long Depression of 1873–1896 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> offered European states an open market and raw materials such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>, cocoa, diamonds, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> also wished to secure the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>, completed in 1869, and navies needed coaling stations and bases.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Seven Western European powers took part: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the United Kingdom. In the Congo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> made treaties with chiefs on behalf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-of-belgium">Leopold II of Belgium</a>, and by 1882 these gave a base for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>. In 1881 Pierre de Brazza raised the French flag over the newly founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a>. Portugal, which claimed the area under old treaties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a>, made a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> on 26 February 1884 to block Leopold's access to the Atlantic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a> of 1884–1885 regulated European colonisation and trade and is seen as emblematic of the scramble.</p><p>The later years of the century saw a move from informal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>, meaning military influence and economic dominance, to direct rule. Many regimes claimed to be suppressing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, the central theme of the Brussels Anti-Slavery Conference of 1889–1890.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By 1914 only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and Liberia remained outside European control, and Ethiopia was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1936. Between 1885 and 1914 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took nearly 30 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>'s population under its control, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> 15 per cent, Portugal 11, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> 9, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> 7 and Italy 1. After the First World War Germany's possessions were divided among Britain, France and Belgium. Rivalries among the imperial powers contributed to the crises that led to the outbreak of the First World War in August 1914.</p><p>Most African colonies became independent during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>. At the conference of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a> in 1964 the new states decided to keep their colonial borders, out of fear of civil wars and regional instability.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Scramble for Africa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It removed the empire’s economic heartland and left Portugal with African territories that it sought to occupy effectively only during the <b>Scramble for Africa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>It fell to the overwhelming technological superiority of British imperial expansion at the height of the <b>Scramble for Africa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…American colonies in 1776; it turned to India, Australia, Asia, Africa and the Pacific, and after 1815 enjoyed a century of almost unchallenged dominance, the Pax Britannica, which was still an almost uninterrupted series of colonial wars, from the Anglo-Burmese and Opium Wars to the <b>Scramble for Africa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/imperialism">Imperialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
